menu "Audio Diagnostics"

config USB_AUDIO_DIAGNOSTICS
	bool "Enable USB audio diagnostics"
	default n
	help
		Compiles in USB audio diagnostic counters and periodic log snapshots.
		When enabled, the firmware tracks ring-buffer fill level, underrun events,
		overrun/drop events, and peak written sample magnitude.

endmenu

menu "USB Audio Behavior"

config USB_AUDIO_BLOCKING_WRITE
    bool "Block render task when USB buffer is full"
    default n
    help
        If enabled, the AMY render task will wait (retry with backoff) until 
        the USB ring buffer has space, effectively slave-clocking the synth
        to the PC's consumption rate. 
        If disabled (default), the task will drop overflowed blocks to
        maintain real-time AMY clock alignment regardless of USB health,
        which can result in 'honky' percussive artifacts if transport is poor.

config OUTPUT_WATCHDOG
    bool "Output level watchdog (UI badge on stuck-loud / clipping output)"
    default y
    help
        Detects output that is continuously at clipping level (~0.5 s) or
        sustained near full scale (~2.5 s, e.g. filter self-oscillation or a
        stuck voice) and shows a small CLIP/LOUD badge in the UI. The check
        peeks at the most recent audio already committed to the USB ring
        buffer from the UI task on Core 0 every 50 ms; the render task and
        Core 1 do no extra work.

config FILTER_SCOPE
    bool "Live filter overlay in the filter editor (filter scope)"
    default y
    help
        Draws a live band on the filter editor's response curve showing where
        the filter cutoff is actually travelling, so EG1 sweeps and filter LFO
        motion are visible instead of having to be inferred from the authored
        numbers.

        This does not sample audio: AMY already computes the modulated cutoff
        as a control-rate scalar once per block, and the render task publishes
        min/max of it into a small cell that the UI drains at 20 Hz. The
        accumulator is armed only while the filter editor is open; when it is
        closed the render task does one load and one branch per block.

endmenu

menu "Render Clock Source"

config RENDER_CLOCK_I2S_ENABLE
    bool "Use I2S DMA backpressure as the render clock (experimental)"
    default n
    help
        Selects an alternate render-clock backend: main/render_clock_i2s.c
        instead of the default main/render_clock.c (GPTimer). The render
        task's timing seam is paced by blocking i2s_channel_write() calls
        to an I2S TX channel configured with every GPIO left unassigned
        (I2S_GPIO_UNUSED), so this changes no pin mux and produces no
        physical signal -- the peripheral's internal clock divider free-runs
        and drains its DMA queue on schedule regardless of GPIO routing,
        and that DMA drain is the only thing this backend uses.

        The I2S TX DMA queue (see RENDER_CLOCK_I2S_DMA_DESC_NUM) gives the
        render task up to (queue depth - 1) block periods of look-ahead
        before a write blocks, trading the GPTimer path's strict zero-slack
        pacing for extra scheduling headroom against a transient overrun.
        One consequence: the GPTimer path's counting overrun signal
        (render_clock_wait() returning >1) does not exist here --
        this backend always returns 1, so s_render_overruns stays 0
        regardless of actual render-task headroom.

        Leave this off (default) to keep the proven GPTimer path fully
        intact. This is a prototype for deliberate, opt-in evaluation, not
        a proven replacement; trace the full clock/DMA impact before
        enabling on hardware.

config RENDER_CLOCK_I2S_DMA_DESC_NUM
    int "I2S TX DMA buffer count (buffer depth)"
    depends on RENDER_CLOCK_I2S_ENABLE
    default 2
    range 2 8
    help
        Number of DMA descriptors (buffers) in the I2S TX ring, each sized
        to exactly one AMY render block (AMY_BLOCK_SIZE frames). This is
        the "double buffer depth" lever: 2 (the default, "double buffering")
        gives one block period (~5.3 ms) of render-task look-ahead before
        i2s_channel_write() blocks; higher values trade more look-ahead
        headroom for more worst-case latency between an actual overrun and
        its effect becoming audible/measurable.

endmenu

# The heap / RTOS / render-timing diagnostic menus moved to
# components/diagnostics/Kconfig, alongside the code they gate. Symbol names
# are unchanged; they now appear under Component config -> Diagnostics.
